var ARTICLES = {
  mps:{tag:"Recurring Revenue",title:"From One-Off Sales to Recurring Revenue: Auto-Replenishment & Managed Print",meta:"Oct 9, 2026 • 5 min read",body:"<p>You don’t need enterprise software to stop selling cartridge-by-cartridge. A lightweight replenishment offer turns “call us when you run out” into “we already shipped it.”</p><h4>The starter version any reseller can offer</h4><ul><li><strong>Par-level agreement:</strong> Agree a minimum stock per printer (e.g. one in the machine + one on the shelf). The customer sends a photo or a quick count monthly — or you check on a scheduled visit.</li><li><strong>Standing order by model:</strong> Record each customer’s printer models and cartridge codes once. Reordering becomes one confirmation, not a new search every time.</li><li><strong>Quarterly cost-per-page review:</strong> Show the customer their print spend per page and where a high-yield switch would save money. This 15-minute review is what justifies staying with you over a cheaper one-off seller.</li></ul><h4>Why it matters</h4><p>Recurring customers order more predictably, complain less about single-box price (because you report on total cost), and rarely shop around — you have made reordering effortless. Start with your five best customers, one printer each. Prove it, then expand.</p><div class='callout'>Keep it honest and simple: no long lock-in needed. “We’ll watch your toner, you can cancel anytime” outsells a complicated contract for most small offices.</div>"},
  stock:{tag:"Inventory",title:"Stock Smart: Fast Movers, Slow Movers & Avoiding Dead Inventory",meta:"Oct 5, 2026 • 6 min read",body:"<p>Two shelves tell the whole story of a reseller’s cash flow: the shelf that empties every week, and the shelf that hasn’t moved since last year. The goal isn’t more stock — it’s the <em>right</em> stock.</p><h4>A simple velocity method</h4><ul><li><strong>Look at the last 90 days of sales</strong> for every SKU. Average daily sales = units sold ÷ 90.</li><li><strong>Days of cover = Stock on hand ÷ Average daily sales.</strong> This tells you how long your current shelf lasts.</li><li><strong>Reorder point = Average daily sales × (lead time + safety days).</strong> When cover falls to your lead time, you are already late.</li></ul><table><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Action</th></tr><tr><td>Cover ≤ lead time</td><td>You will run out before new stock arrives</td><td>Order now</td></tr><tr><td>Cover ≤ 30 days</td><td>Healthy but thinning</td><td>Plan the reorder</td></tr><tr><td>No sales in 90 days</td><td>Dead or seasonal stock</td><td>Stop reordering; clear deliberately</td></tr></table><h4>Know your families</h4><p>Fast movers cluster by installed printer base — high-volume Brother and HP laser families, for example, turn far more reliably than a niche model. Use a printer-compatibility / Ink &amp; Toner Finder approach: if many printers in your customers’ offices share a cartridge family, that family deserves depth; one-off models deserve one unit, or order-on-demand.</p><div class='callout'><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> Depth on your top 20% of SKUs, breadth of one on the long tail, and a hard stop on anything with zero sales in 90 days until you know why.</div>"},
  building:{tag:"Sales & Accounts",title:"Win the Whole Building: Expanding One Happy Account Into Every Department",meta:"Sep 28, 2026 • 5 min read",body:"<p>Ask most resellers who their best customer is and they’ll name a company. Ask how many printers that company actually runs, and the answer is usually a guess. That gap — between the printers you supply and the printers in the building — is the cheapest growth available to you, because there is no acquisition cost: the trust is already there.</p><h4>Step 1: Build the fleet map</h4><ul><li>For each of your top 10 accounts, list every printer you know of: model, location in the building, and which cartridge it takes.</li><li>Fill the gaps on your next delivery or service visit — walk the floor, note the models by the photocopier, in admin, in the back office.</li><li>Record it once, properly. A customer whose printers you have mapped is a customer a competitor has to work much harder to take.</li></ul><h4>Step 2: Ask the expansion question</h4><p>Don’t pitch — ask: <em>“We look after the two printers in reception. Who supplies the ones upstairs? If it’s easier, we can put them all on the same order.”</em> You are offering convenience, not asking for charity. Most offices split suppliers by accident, not by decision — different people ordered at different times. Consolidation is a favour to them: one invoice, one delivery, one phone call.</p><h4>Step 3: Make the second printer effortless</h4><ul><li>Add the new models to the account’s standing list so reordering is one confirmation.</li><li>Offer a par-level (one in the machine, one on the shelf) for the highest-volume new printer first.</li><li>Report back after a quarter: pages supplied, cost per page, stock-outs avoided. That short report is what turns “the toner person” into “our print supplier.”</li></ul><div class='callout'><strong>The math that motivates:</strong> an account supplying 2 of its 6 printers is giving you a third of its print spend. Winning two more printers can grow that account by half — without a single cold call.</div>"},
  quote:{tag:"Sales & Accounts",title:"Quote Fast, Follow Up Twice: The Response-Time Habits That Win Orders",meta:"Sep 21, 2026 • 4 min read",body:"<p>When an office asks three suppliers for a price, the order usually goes to whoever replies first with a complete, confident quote — even when they are not the cheapest. The buyer has a printer blinking at them and a job to get back to. Speed is a feature of your service, and it costs you nothing but routine.</p><h4>The same-day quote routine</h4><ul><li><strong>Acknowledge within the hour,</strong> even if the full quote needs checking: “Got it — pricing the TN-760 and the drum for you, back to you by 3.” An acknowledged request stops the customer shopping further while they wait.</li><li><strong>Quote complete:</strong> cartridge, yield, cost per page, stock status, and delivery timing in one message. A quote that triggers three follow-up questions loses to one that answers them.</li><li><strong>Quote the pair:</strong> if they asked for toner, add the drum or the high-yield option as a one-line alternative. You are not upselling — you are saving them a second order.</li></ul><h4>The two-touch follow-up</h4><ul><li><strong>Touch one (next business day):</strong> short and useful — confirm stock is still held, restate delivery day. No pressure language.</li><li><strong>Touch two (three to four days later):</strong> add something new — a high-yield comparison, a compatible option at a lower cost per page, or a note that the printer’s drum is near its rated life too. A follow-up that only asks “any update?” teaches them to ignore you; one that adds information teaches them to open your emails.</li></ul><div class='callout'>Track it simply: date asked, date quoted, date followed up, won or lost. After a month you will see your real pattern — most resellers find the losses cluster where the first reply took more than a day.</div>"},
  margin:{tag:"Pricing & Margin",title:"Protect the Margin: Discount With a Reason, Never by Habit",meta:"Sep 14, 2026 • 5 min read",body:"<p>A discount given for no reason is not generosity — it is training. The customer learns that your first price is negotiable, asks every time, and your margin quietly becomes whatever survives the conversation. Protecting margin isn’t about being rigid; it’s about making every concession earn something back.</p><h4>When a discount is justified</h4><ul><li><strong>Volume that changes your cost picture:</strong> a genuinely larger or more predictable order — a standing monthly quantity, a full fleet on one invoice.</li><li><strong>Commitment:</strong> an account moving all its printers to you, or onto auto-replenishment, is worth more than one order at full price.</li><li><strong>A real competitive situation:</strong> a documented quote from a comparable supplier on a comparable product — not “somewhere online was cheaper.”</li></ul><h4>Trade, don’t give</h4><p>Every concession should swap for something: a lower price in exchange for the whole building’s printers; a discount for payment on order instead of terms; a better unit price when they take the high-yield version that costs you less to handle per page. The customer still wins — and so do you, which is why the arrangement lasts.</p><h4>The honest alternatives to a discount</h4><ul><li><strong>Offer the compatible or remanufactured option</strong> at its real, lower cost per page — a genuine saving, not a margin cut.</li><li><strong>Offer the high-yield version:</strong> more upfront, less per page. Many “price” objections are really cost-per-page objections wearing a disguise.</li><li><strong>Say no, politely, with the reason:</strong> “At that price I’d have to cut the replacement guarantee — and that guarantee is why your last three orders were worry-free.” Customers respect a floor that is explained; they test floors that are silent.</li></ul><div class='callout'><strong>Do the annual math once:</strong> a habitual 5% off across an account’s year of orders often costs more than the profit on their two biggest orders combined. Small percentages, applied everywhere, are how margins actually disappear.</div>"},
  checkin:{tag:"Retention & Referrals",title:"The 15-Minute Monthly Check-In That Keeps Accounts From Drifting",meta:"Sep 7, 2026 • 4 min read",body:"<p>Resellers rarely lose an account in a single dramatic moment. They lose it in silence: a new office manager arrives, a competitor’s rep happens to call on the week a cartridge failed, an order goes to whoever answered first — and one day the regular order simply doesn’t come. Accounts don’t usually leave you; they drift. The check-in is how you notice the drift while it’s still cheap to fix.</p><h4>What the 15 minutes covers</h4><ul><li><strong>Stock:</strong> “How are levels looking — anything close to empty?” You are preventing the emergency order that goes to whoever is fastest that day.</li><li><strong>Problems:</strong> “Any print quality grumbles since last month?” A streaky page mentioned casually in a check-in is a saved account; the same problem unmentioned becomes a reason to try someone else.</li><li><strong>Changes:</strong> new staff, a new printer, a busier season coming. Every change is either a risk or an order — the check-in is how you find out which.</li><li><strong>One useful thing:</strong> a firmware heads-up, a high-yield saving for their busiest printer, a reminder their drum is near rated life. Arrive as information, not interruption.</li></ul><h4>Make it a system, not a mood</h4><p>Put every active account on a monthly rotation — the same week each month, a short call or email, logged in one line: date, anything flagged, follow-up done. Fifteen minutes per account sounds like a lot until you compare it with the cost of winning the account back, or replacing it. New accounts are the most expensive revenue there is; keeping one is the cheapest.</p><div class='callout'>The check-in also protects your recurring revenue: par-level and standing-order accounts stay on the programme when they feel looked after — and quietly cancel when contact stops.</div>"},
  local:{tag:"Retention & Referrals",title:"Get Found by Offices Near You: Referrals, Reviews & Local Visibility",meta:"Aug 31, 2026 • 5 min read",body:"<p>Print supplies are a local business wearing an online disguise. Offices want a supplier who can answer the phone, deliver fast, and take a problem cartridge back without a ticket number. Your advantage over a distant listing is proximity — this guide is about making sure the offices near you actually know it.</p><h4>Referrals: the ask is the whole strategy</h4><ul><li>Happy customers rarely refer unprompted — not from ingratitude, but because it never crosses their mind. Ask, specifically: <em>“Do you know another office nearby that deals with the toner headache? Happy to help them the same way.”</em></li><li>Ask right after a save — the emergency delivery, the warranty swap handled without fuss. That is the moment your value is most obvious to them.</li><li>Make it easy to pass along: a named contact, a direct number, one line about what you do. Then thank the referrer when the account orders — every time.</li></ul><h4>Reviews &amp; local presence</h4><ul><li>Keep your Google Business Profile complete and current — hours, service area, photos of the shop or warehouse, and the categories offices actually search for.</li><li>Ask satisfied accounts for a review in the same conversation as the referral ask: “A short review helps other offices find us.” A handful of specific, recent reviews (“delivered same day when our printer died”) outweighs a wall of generic five stars.</li><li>Be visible where offices already gather: the local business association, the printer-repair shop that doesn’t sell supplies (and vice versa), the IT freelancer who gets asked “who does your toner?” once a month.</li></ul><h4>One page that does the work</h4><p>If your website has one page aimed at new offices, make it answer the three questions they actually ask: Do you stock for my printer? How fast can I get it? What happens if a cartridge fails? A page that answers those plainly — like the Finder approach of starting from the printer model — converts proximity into orders.</p><div class='callout'><strong>Sequence it:</strong> one referral ask per happy delivery this month beats a marketing campaign you’ll start “someday.” Local growth compounds quietly, the same way drift does — the only question is which direction.</div>"}
